  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 251 PM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nSynoptically, on Sunday morning an upper level trough looks to begin \nmoving through the Tri-State Area, potentially with an associated \nweak jet streak. Come Monday, the trough begins to move eastward and \nout of the area. Our upper level flow should then return to come \nfrom the west until around Tuesday night into Wednesday morning.\n\nHigh temperatures for the long term look to remain cooler than \nprevious days, with highs mainly in the 70s. The warmest day of the \nperiod looks to be Wednesday with highs in the upper 70s to low 80s \nfor the majority of the CWA. Winds are forecast to be light and \nvariable throughout the late weekend into the early work week. \nDuring this period, there are chances for the majority of the \narea to see typical afternoon showers/storms. Sunday currently \nlooks to have the greatest chance of seeing precipitation. NBM \nguidance shows around a 30-60% chance of exceeding 0.1 inches of\nprecip from the early morning, and decreasing in the afternoon.\nMonday through Wednesday the Probability of Precipitation is \naround 20-40% each afternoon. Guidance indicates lower values of\nCAPE and moderate shear, which would lean more towards showers \nthan severe storms. \n\nBeginning Tuesday night into Wednesday morning, uncertainty in the \nforecast begins to increase. For Tuesday night, guidance is \nhinting at troughing in the Western CONUS. By Wednesday morning,\nan upper level ridge is expected to be located over the \nSouthern US / Gulf region, with signs of a weak trough moving \nthrough the Northern Plains. The primary uncertainty lies in how\nstrong the high will be, where its center will locate, and \nwhether the weak trough to the west moves quickly, slowly, or \nremains nearly stationary. Guidance indicates the center of the \nridge could be as far north as the AR/MO/OK border or as far \nsouth as the Gulf. Certain models show the trough sweeping \nthrough in a single day, while others keep it near stationary \nover the AZ/CA/NV border. The exact locations of the ridge and \ntrough will impact our precipitation chances for Wednesday. It \nwill be interesting to see how guidance handles this upper level\npattern uncertainty as we get closer.",
